<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cersobleptes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 359–341 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Kersebleptes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cersobleptes was a son of Cotys I and the most powerful of the three kings among whom the Odrysian Kingdom was divided in 360 BCE, ruling its eastern part. Aided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> commander Charidemus, he contested the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Thracian Chersonese</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> before ceding it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> defeated him in 342–341 BCE and annexed most of his lands, ending Odrysian independence for a time.</p></div>
